.TH SPRITE2DTEST 1
.SH NAME
sprite2dtest \- check that draw3d's sprite2d places pixels exactly
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sprite2dtest
[
.I provider
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.IR Draw3d (2)'s
.I sprite2d
is a rotating, scaling resample, and three of its cases have an answer
that does not depend on the resampler at all: at 1:1 with no rotation it
must reproduce the source exactly, a quarter turn must reproduce the
source turned by hand, and doubling must turn every source pixel into a
2\(mu2 block.
.I Sprite2dtest
renders both through
.I sprite2d
and through a plain masked
.IR draw ,
which is the oracle, and compares every pixel.
It covers square and oblong sources from 7 to 207 pixels, with and
without a mask; quarter, half and three-quarter turns; and doublings.
The three exercise different code:
1:1 takes the identity blit,
the turns take the general rotated loop,
and only the doublings reach the separable unrotated path that every
scaled call without a rotation goes through.
.PP
A fourth family draws into the
.I window
instead of an offscreen image, and it is there for a specific reason:
the GPU sprite path in
.I emu
takes the screen or a fully-clear layer of it and nothing else, so every
case above misses it entirely and the whole
.B J
message could be broken while the test passed.
The on-screen family covers the same sizes and quarter turns, reads the
result back off the display's own screen image \- a
.I readpixels
and specifically not a
.IR draw ,
which GPU output is invisible to \- and compares it against the same
plain masked draw done into the same window, so the border and the layer
offset cancel.
It compares red, green and blue only: the screen is
.B XBGR32
and nothing displays the fourth byte, on which
.I memdraw
and the Metal blend disagree.
.PP
Deliberately offsetting the GPU sprite by three pixels fails all forty
on-screen cases and none of the others, which is what establishes that
the family reaches the GPU at all.
.PP
.I Provider
is the implementation to test, by path; the default is
.BR /dis/math/draw3d.dis .
Give an older or deliberately altered one to run the same cases as a
control.
.PP
The last line is
.B PASS
or
.BR FAIL .
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
emu\-cocoa \-r. /dis/sprite2dtest.dis
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/sprite2dtest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR draw3d (2),
.IR draw3dbench (1)
.SH BUGS
It needs a window, so it runs under
.I emu\-cocoa
and not
.IR emu\-g .
.PP
Only the three cases with a resampler-independent answer are checked.
An arbitrary angle or scale has no oracle here, so a resample that is
exact at a quarter turn and wrong at thirty degrees would pass.
.PP
Only
.I sprite2d
is covered.
The
.I sprite3
family shares the same transform but reaches it by a different route and
is not tested; the mask-edge cases in
.IR draw3dbench (1)
are what exercise it.
.PP
The on-screen family draws one sprite at the middle of the window and
compares the whole window, so it would not catch a second sprite drawn
somewhere the first was not.
